## Performance Checklist

Before committing performance-sensitive code:
- [ ] No allocations in `_Process()` or `_PhysicsProcess()`
- [ ] All entities use object pooling (bullets, enemies, particles)
- [ ] Node references are cached (no repeated `GetNode()`)
- [ ] Groups are cached (no `GetNodesInGroup()` in hot paths)
- [ ] Rate limiters prevent spawn spikes
- [ ] Graceful degradation under high load
- [ ] Integer math used for counters and indices
- [ ] Trigonometry pre-calculated or cached
- [ ] Distance checks use `DistanceSquaredTo()` when possible
- [ ] Shader operations minimized per-pixel
- [ ] Particle systems use GPU particles (>20 particles)
- [ ] Debug code is gated behind constants
- [ ] Profiled with 500+ bullets on screen
- [ ] Maintains 60 FPS during stress test

## Common Performance Pitfalls

### 1. String Concatenation in Loops
```csharp
// ❌ BAD: String allocation every iteration
public string BuildDebugString()
{
    string result = "";
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++)
    {
        result += i + " "; // Allocates new string each time!
    }
    return result;
}

// ✅ GOOD: Use StringBuilder or string.Join
public string BuildDebugString()
{
    var parts = new List<string>();
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++)
    {
        parts.Add(i.ToString());
    }
    return string.Join(" ", parts);
}
```

### 2. Unnecessary Collision Layer Checks
```csharp
// ❌ BAD: Checking every object for collision
public override void _PhysicsProcess(double delta)
{
    foreach (var body in GetTree().GetNodesInGroup("all_objects"))
    {
        if (body.Overlaps(this))
        {
            HandleCollision(body);
        }
    }
}

// ✅ GOOD: Use collision layers and Area2D signals
public override void _Ready()
{
    CollisionLayer = 1; // Player bullet layer
    CollisionMask = 2;  // Enemy layer only
    BodyEntered += OnBodyEntered;
}

private void OnBodyEntered(Node2D body)
{
    // Only called for relevant collisions
    HandleCollision(body);
}
```

### 3. Excessive Signal Emissions
```csharp
// ❌ BAD: Emitting signals every frame
public override void _Process(double delta)
{
    PositionChanged?.Invoke(Position); // 60 times per second!
}

// ✅ GOOD: Emit only on significant changes
private Vector2 _lastEmittedPos = Vector2.Zero;
private const float EmitThreshold = 5.0f;

public override void _Process(double delta)
{
    if (Position.DistanceSquaredTo(_lastEmittedPos) > EmitThreshold * EmitThreshold)
    {
        PositionChanged?.Invoke(Position);
        _lastEmittedPos = Position;
    }
}
```

## Summary

Performance is CRITICAL for bullet-hell gameplay. Always profile with realistic load (500+ bullets), use object pooling religiously, cache everything, and degrade gracefully under stress. When in doubt, measure first, optimize second.